-> Spring Caching :
    - Caching is an extra layer to store the data temporarily. It works fast as there will be no database call.
        For first request the data is fetched and stored in cache.
        for second request, the data is served from the cache.
            If data is updated , cache too gets updated.
            Cache - temporary memory, Cache memory too gets filled,
            Time is Configurable i.e. we can decide how much time the data to stay. which is know as TTL (Time to Live)
    - Spring Cache internally uses Concurrent HashMap
    - Other cache providers are -
        Caffeine : it is a local cache. This stores the data in application memory. 3rd party implementation
        Ehcache : 3rd party
        Redis cache : Distributed cache. It is also a 3rd party provider.
                        It needs a separate server
    - To work with it we need to add Spring Cache dependency
    - Spring Cache is Specification.
        - caffeine gives implementation;
            - To use Caffeine cache, we need to add dependency
        - ehcache gives implementation;
        - redis gives implementation
          else if we didn't provide any 3rd party provider, Spring Boot gives default implementation with HashMap
    - @CachePut(value = "", key = "") - will update the cache
    - @Cacheable
    - @CacheEvict(value = "", key = "") - to delete
    - (Whenever we update, we delete the old cache and we store a new cache. This is called Cache Invalidation.)
    - When we call Cache, CacheManager gets created in background by SpringBoot which handles keys and values.
        But if we use 3rd party provider(caffeine, etc.), we have to manage CacheManager
        create a config class for CacheConfig which consists of CacheManager method in it.

